<p>Many an internet cinephile has wondered aloud if the Criterion Closet is a real place, or just a set thrown together to make videos when famous directors like <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2026/07/christopher-nolan-picks-15-favorite-films-from-the-criterion-closet.html">Christopher Nolan</a>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/?p=1121369">Francis Ford Coppola</a>, or <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2026/03/jim-jarmusch-picks-his-favorite-films-from-the-the-criterion-collection.html">Jim Jarmusch</a> drop by. It turns out indeed to exist, not only at Criterion’s offices in New York, but also elsewhere. The <a href="https://www.criterion.com/current/posts/8602-what-to-expect-at-the-criterion-mobile-closet?srsltid=AfmBOopnmue-dmwoul1OT59peI6XNgI0wBFdO4PXCcpA5GA5yXbQozP4">Criterion Mobile Closet</a>, for example, has taken the label’s home-video library on the road since 2024, the 40th anniversary of the company’s foundation. If it comes to your town, queue up in as timely a fashion as possible, and you may get the chance to enter and remain inside for three minutes. That may sound brief, but no less an auteur than Wes Anderson takes almost a minute less to make his cinematic selections in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJXKHJeVav0">the new video above</a>.</p>
<p>The occasion was the Criterion Mobile Closet’s stop at the Hollywood Bowl, which coincided with the three-night concert event <a href="https://www.hollywoodbowl.com/events/performances/4288/2026-07-10/music-from-the-films-of-wes-anderson#">Music from the Films of Wes Anderson</a> this past June. With a few days’ travel ahead of him, he picks out nine&nbsp;Criterion releases&nbsp;as viewing material to take along on “our road trip across America.”</p>
