</div>

<p>With an eye toward pleasing the entire family, he goes mostly for “reliable, standby” classics, beginning with Alfred Hitchcock’s <em>The 39 Steps</em>&nbsp;and continuing on to Michael Powell and <span class="ytAttributedStringHost ytAttributedStringWhiteSpacePreWrap" dir="auto"><span class="ytAttributedStringLinkInheritColor" dir="auto">Emeric Pressburger</span></span>’s&nbsp;<em>The Red Shoes </em>(“my daughter loves this movie”), and two from Ernst Lubitsch,&nbsp;<em>Heaven Can Wait</em> and&nbsp;<em>Design for Living&nbsp;</em>— a picture that must appeal to Anderson on several levels, not least in that it deals with Americans in Paris, a set of which he’s long been a member.</p>
<p>Other of Anderson’s more canonical picks include Preston Sturges’ <em>The Lady Eve </em>with Barbara Stanwyck, “one of the greatest actresses in the history of cinema, as everybody knows”; <em>Stagecoach</em>, which requires by way of introduction only the name of John Ford; and, from Satyajit Ray<em>, </em>not the celebrated Apu trilogy (which Nolan took home) but <em>Charulata</em>, the inspiration for the binocular shots in his own <em>Moonrise Kingdom</em>. He also pulls from the shelf Sydney Pollack’s <em>Tootsie</em> (“remember, Bill Murray is in there”), a somewhat more recent selection. What stands out even more is the practically brand new&nbsp;<em>Flow</em>, Latvian animator Gints Zilbalodis’ dialogue-free adventure movie with only animal characters. It may have yet to pass the test of time, but if the man who directed <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2018/12/astonishing-sushi-scene-wes-andersons-isle-dogs-animated-time-lapse-month-long-shoot.html"><em>Isle of Dogs</em></a> likes it — to say nothing of Criterion’s imprimatur — it’s surely worth a watch.</p>
